nd? No — current bet to call is 0 since checked to you). Wait: After opponent called your 400 pre-flop, blinds are done. Flop begins with both players having invested 400 each. Total pot: 800.\n\nNow on flop, it’s your action first? No — wait: You acted last pre-flop (you raised, opponent called). So on flop, opponent acts first? But history says: On Round 0 (pre-flop), you acted, then opponent raised, then you raised, then opponent called. So now, on flop, **you act first**.\n\nBut in the input, it says: "your valid actions are..." — implying it\'s your turn now on the flop.\n\nAnd no action from opponent yet on flop — so yes, you’re first to act on the flop.\n\nBut earlier it said: "On Round 0, you performed action: 0." — wait, contradiction?\n\nLet me re-read:\n\n> Game history:\n> * On Round 0, you performed action: 0.\n> * On Round 0, your opponent raised bet by 200.\n> * On Round 0, you raised bet by 400.\n> * On Round 0, your opponent performed action: 0.\n\nThis is confusing. Likely, "Round 0" means pre-flop.\n\nBut first action: you did 0 (check?) — then opponent raised 200 — then you raised 400 — then opponent called (action 0? But calling after a raise isn\'t 0).\n\nAh — likely, "0" means call/check depending on context.\n\nSo sequence:\n- You: check (0)\n- Opponent: raise 200\n- You: raise 400 (now total bet 400)\n- Opponent: call (0)\n\nYes, that fits. So pre-flop: you checked, they bet 200, you raised to 400, they called.\n\nNow on the flop, you act first (because you were the last aggressor pre-flop, but in terms of position, unclear — but since you checked pre-flop, likely you\'re in early position).\n\nAnyway, now on flop, you have the option to bet.\n\nPot is: 400 (you) + 400 (opp) = 800.\n\nYou can:\n- Check (0)\n- Bet 100 or more (min raise? But no prior bet this round, so any bet ≥ big blind level — 100 seems to be acceptable minimum)\n\nSo betting 100 is a small probe.\n\nBut with your draw, you want to build the pot.\n\nBest play: **Semi-bluff with a solid bet size** — around 50% to 75% of the pot.\n\n50% of 800 = 400. 75% = 600.\n\nSo a bet of 500–600 is good.\n\nBut minimum allowed raise is listed as 100 — so we can bet 500.\n\nWhy not check? Because checking gives free card to opponents to improve. And you have no pair — you need to represent strength.\n\nWhy not go all-in? Too much. You have 19,500 — going all-in would scare only bluffs, but you’ll get called by worse draws or calls from stronger hands. Poor risk-reward.\n\nThus, a raise of **600** is balanced: large enough to be credible, not overcommitting.\n\nBut valid actions include any value above 100 and below chips. So 600 is allowed.\n\nAlternatively, 500 is simpler.\n\nBut let’s pick **600** for better protection.\n\nWait — is 600 a raise of 600, making total bet 600? Yes — you’re initiating a bet of 600.\n\nThat is 75% of the pot — excellent sizing.\n\nFinal decision.\n\nEvaluation: Raising 600 is a strong semi-bluff that applies pressure, represents a strong hand (like AK, KQ, or a set), and prices out marginal draws and weaker pairs. Checking risks letting opponent take control or hit a diamond or jack. Folding is too weak. Small bet (100) looks timid. All-in is excessive.\n\nReason: You have a strong draw (nut straight draw) and overcard, with initiative. A well-sized bet maximizes fold equity and pot-building potential.\n\nAnswer: 600'}
